I ran a Node.js trace question on myself and got it wrong, in two specific ways that separate reading the docs from actually being able to explain the thing under pressure.
Press enter or click to view image in full size

The question
console.log('Start');setTimeout(() => {
console.log('setTimeout');
}, 0);
setImmediate(() => {
console.log('setImmediate');
});
process.nextTick(() => {
console.log('nextTick');
});
Promise.resolve().then(() => {
console.log('Promise');
});
setTimeout(() => {
process.nextTick(() => {
console.log('nextTick');
});
}, 0);
console.log('End');
My answer:
Start
End
nextTick
Promise
setImmediate
setTimeout
nextTickTwo things wrong with it.
Mistake 1: setImmediate vs setTimeout
I had setImmediate running before setTimeout. Reversed. When both are scheduled from synchronous top-level code, setTimeout(fn, 0) almost always wins in practice. By the time the loop actually gets moving, more time has usually passed than the 0ms delay, so the timers phase (which runs before the check phase, where setImmediate lives) already finds its callback ready.
But that’s not a guarantee, it’s a race that timers usually win on real hardware. The only place the order is actually deterministic is when setImmediate gets scheduled from inside an I/O callback. There, poll hands off straight to check with nothing in between, and setImmediate wins every time, no exceptions.
Mistake 2: dropping a nextTick
The second setTimeout schedules a process.nextTick() inside itself. I filed that mentally as "happens later, batched with the rest," which is wrong. process.nextTick() doesn't wait for anything. It drains right after whatever's currently running finishes, before the loop is allowed to move to the next phase. It doesn't matter that it was nested three levels deep instead of called at the top.
Correct order:
Start
End
nextTick
Promise
setTimeout
nextTick
setImmediateRebuilding the model
Fixing the trace wasn’t the useful part. What actually fixed my understanding was trying to restate the rule myself and getting that wrong too, twice.
First pass, I tried explaining microtasks as living inside specific phases, nextTick and Promise callbacks running inside Poll, the nested nextTick running inside Pending Callbacks. Wrong. Microtasks aren’t inside any phase. They’re a checkpoint between phases and between individual callbacks, independent of which phase just ran. I’d been treating “the loop has six phases” and “microtasks slot in somewhere on that list” as the same category of thing. They’re not. One is a queue-processing structure. The other is a priority interrupt around that structure.
Second pass, trying to compress it further: sync code, then microtask, then macrotask, then phases start. Still off by one concept. Macrotask and “phases starting” aren’t sequential, they’re the same event from two angles. A macrotask is just a callback sitting in a phase’s queue. Running one means a phase is currently executing. There’s no separate macrotask step that happens before phases begin.
What actually held up:
Sync code runs (pre-loop, call stack)
thenMicrotask checkpoint drains: nextTick fully, then Promise fully
then
Event loop cycles: Timers, Pending Callbacks, Idle/Prepare,
Poll, Check, Close Callbacks, back to Timers
(each phase's queue holds macrotasks, running one means a phase is executing)
then
after every single macrotask callback, microtask checkpoint runs again
then
repeat until no pending work is left
Three separate things, not four: synchronous execution, the microtask checkpoint (a mechanism, not a phase), and the six-phase loop where macrotasks actually run. Once I split those apart cleanly, the ordering questions stopped being memorization and turned into something I could derive on the spot, including the exact question I’d gotten wrong originally.
Proving it with a script
Reading the corrected model was one thing, running it was another. So I wrote a script to hit every phase deliberately:
'use strict';
const fs = require('fs');
const net = require('net');// --- PRE-LOOP: synchronous code ---
console.log('1. SYNC: top-level code runs first');
// --- MICROTASKS: not a phase, drains right after sync code ---
process.nextTick(() => console.log('2. MICROTASK: nextTick'));
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3. MICROTASK: Promise'));
// --- TIMERS phase ---
setTimeout(() => {
console.log('4. TIMERS phase: setTimeout(0) fired');
}, 0);
// --- CHECK phase (top-level setImmediate) ---
setImmediate(() => {
console.log('7. CHECK phase: setImmediate fired (top-level, order vs setTimeout not guaranteed)');
});
// --- POLL phase: real I/O callback ---
fs.readFile(__filename, () => {
console.log('5. POLL phase: fs.readFile callback (real I/O)');
// Scheduled from INSIDE an I/O callback: this ordering IS guaranteed.
// Poll queue is empty right after this, setImmediate is pending,
// so poll hands off to check immediately, before the next Timers phase.
setImmediate(() => {
console.log('6. CHECK phase (from within I/O): guaranteed to run before the next setTimeout below');
});
setTimeout(() => {
console.log('8. TIMERS phase (later lap): setTimeout scheduled from inside I/O');
}, 0);
});
// --- CLOSE CALLBACKS phase ---
const server = net.createServer();
server.listen(0, () => {
server.close(() => {
console.log('9. CLOSE CALLBACKS phase: server "close" event');
});
});
// --- PENDING CALLBACKS phase (best-effort only) ---
// Some OS-level errors (e.g. ECONNREFUSED from a raw TCP connect) can have
// their callback deferred to the pending callbacks phase on the next loop
// tick rather than firing immediately. This is platform-dependent and Node
// gives no direct hook to prove which phase handled it, included here for
// completeness, not as reliable proof of phase attribution.
const badSocket = net.connect(1, '127.0.0.1');
badSocket.on('error', () => {
console.log('X. ERROR callback: may be deferred to PENDING CALLBACKS phase on some platforms (not reliably observable)');
});
// NOTE: IDLE/PREPARE phase is internal-only, libuv uses it to set up the
// poll phase. There is no userland API to schedule a callback into it,
// so it cannot be demonstrated with a code example, only named.
Ran it, and the model held. Timers and the top-level setImmediate raced, non-deterministic, flipped order across runs. The setImmediate nested inside fs.readFile fired before its sibling setTimeout every single time, exactly as predicted.
Two phases I couldn’t produce output for. Idle/Prepare is internal only, libuv uses it to set up Poll, no userland API touches it. Pending Callbacks is real, but the textbook trigger (deferred TCP errors like ECONNREFUSED, platform-dependent) isn’t something Node lets you prove from your own code with certainty. I left both in the script labeled as such instead of faking a console.log I couldn’t actually back up.
That distinction turned out to matter more than the trace itself. Reciting six phase names is easy. Knowing which two you can’t force from application code, and being able to say why, is a different signal. It means the model got tested, not just repeated.
What actually changed
Nothing about the event loop is new information here, it’s all in the Node docs. What changed is how I hold it. Before tonight I could recite the phase list. I couldn’t derive an ordering I hadn’t already memorized, which is exactly what a wrong answer to one practice question exposed in about ten seconds.
The fix wasn’t reading more. It was getting the trace wrong, then trying to explain the correction in my own words twice and watching both explanations fail for different, specific reasons, then writing code that could only pass if the model was actually right. Each wrong attempt removed one incorrect assumption I didn’t know I was carrying. By the time the script ran clean, there was nothing left to memorize because there was nothing left I couldn’t derive from first principles.
That’s the part worth taking from this if you’re prepping for anything technical under time pressure: a concept you can recite and a concept you can rebuild under a wrong answer are not the same skill, and only one of them survives being asked a follow-up question you didn’t prepare for.
